#lang saffron
;; saffron-lang-plan S6: a `(Vec any)` element used as a DYNAMIC OPERAND -- as
;; an `if` condition, in arithmetic, in a comparison, in `and`.
;;
;; None of these compiled.  A `(Vec any)` element is stored boxed, so the
;; generic accessor hands its result back as the boxed slot's carrier WORD, and
;; an operand whose static type is already `any` gets no widen from
;; `elab_coerce_to_any` -- so the emitted C was `__tur_dyn_truthy(<int64_t>)`
;; against a `tur_tagged_t` parameter, and the arith and compare helpers the
;; same way.  A hard cc error, not a wrong answer; the interpreter was fine
;; throughout, so this was codegen only.
;;
;; This is the THIRD position needing the same bridge, after the `any` readers
;; (`type-of`, `any-is?`, `cast`) and an argument handed to a parameter declared
;; `any`.  All three now go through `emit_any_from_carrier`; the general fix --
;; normalise once at PRODUCTION rather than at each consumer -- is recorded in
;; docs/reported/any-carrier-straddle-is-bridged-per-consumer.md.
;;
;; The truthiness answers are D4's rule, not a typo: ONLY nil and `false` are
;; falsy, so an empty string and a zero are both truthy.  Both are in here
;; because "" and 0 are exactly what a reader coming from another dynamic
;; language would expect to be falsy.
;;
;; 7.1 rather than 7.0: `(+ 1 7.1)` printing 8 instead of 8.1 would say the
;; operand truncated on its way through the box.

(defn main []
  (let [v [1 "" 7.1 0]]
    (println (if (vec-get v 0) "t0" "f0"))
    (println (if (vec-get v 1) "t1" "f1"))
    (println (if (vec-get v 3) "t3" "f3"))
    (println (+ (vec-get v 0) (vec-get v 2)))
    (println (> (vec-get v 2) (vec-get v 0)))
    (println (and (vec-get v 0) (vec-get v 2)))
    (vec-free v))
  0)
